#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222376} :activity/id #uuid "80e6356e-9b75-58b6-9da9-f8c9047c131d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048443 :topic/id #uuid "9fcc2c1e-7bc0-5b8e-a825-9ff84b01fab5" :topic/math-academy-id 6382} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222376 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222514 :tutorial/content "When a function has period $2L$ (instead of $2\\pi$), the \"usual\" Fourier formulas still work, but the sine and cosine inputs must be rescaled so that each term completes an integer number of oscillations over one period. \n\nWe do this by converting the $2L$-periodic function into a $2\\pi$-periodic one using a change of variables.\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2L$-periodic extension of a function $F(x)$ from $x\\in[-L,L)$ to $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\nTo use the standard $2\\pi$-period Fourier formulas, define\n\n$$\ns=\\frac{\\pi}{L}x\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ng(s)=f\\!\\left(\\frac{L}{\\pi}s\\right).\n$$\n\nThen $g$ is $2\\pi$-periodic.\n\nFor a $2\\pi$-periodic function $g$, the Fourier series is\n\n$$\ng(s)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\bigl(a_n\\cos(ns)+b_n\\sin(ns)\\bigr),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,ds, \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}(ns)\\,ds,\\,n\\ge 1, \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}(ns)\\,ds,\\,n\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $g(s)=f\\!\\left(\\dfrac{L}{\\pi}s\\right)$ and $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{L}x$.\nFirst, for $a_n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}(ns)\\,ds \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\,(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\\operatorname{cos}(ns)\\,ds \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f\\,(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)cos\\,(n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\,d\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCoefficients $b_n$ (for $n \\geq 1$) and $a_0$ can be found similarly.\n\nTo summarize, the Fourier series of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\frac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left(a_n\\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)+b_n\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the coefficients are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,dx \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,dx,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,dx,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's see some concrete examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "5bb98db0-8e34-5cf2-8911-56a6971740ab" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14681 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0045b15e-7a1f-5142-94c5-082ec814b34d" :step/math-academy-id 601031 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222377}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222377 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221443 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221196 :question/id #uuid "b8658f47-7f42-530a-a62e-1cc3144059aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-21835"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12ff3452-19b6-566a-8b39-ad880dee8858" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050676}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317177}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Effects of the Horizontal Scaling on Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "0b4fe87b-2102-5820-bc57-996b33b5332d" :step/math-academy-id 601032 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222378}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222378 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221444 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221197 :question/id #uuid "ba1cc59e-66ed-5